---
/**
 * foodcraft / FooterBN — "Panday Coffee Works" · THE COLOPHON.
 *
 * ROLE: the last place a visitor looks for the name, the number, the address
 * and the way back into the page before they give up. So every cell here is
 * real owner data or the cell is absent.
 *
 * WHAT THE DESIGN DRAWS — reproduced value for value from the element carrying
 * data-component="FOOTER" in "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html", nothing
 * re-derived:
 *   footer          display:grid, minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr), NO bottom rule
 *                                                    → the wrapper's .sec
 *   left column     border-right 1px #C8C7C2, and NOTHING inside it
 *                                                    → the wrapper's .rail
 *   right column    no padding of its own; carries two blocks:
 *     grid          minmax(0,1.6fr) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr), NO gap,
 *                   border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2, cells 1 and 2 opened by a
 *                   border-right 1px #C8C7C2
 *       cell 1      padding 40px 34px · name 800/22px/-.02em/uppercase #17181A
 *                   · meta mono 10px/.16em/uppercase #A8351B margin 7px 0 15px
 *                   · blurb 14px/1.8 #3C3E42 margin 0 0 16px max-width 36ch
 *                   · action mono 10.5px/.14em/uppercase #A8351B with a "→"
 *       cell 2      padding 40px 30px · heading mono 10px/.2em/uppercase
 *                   #5A5C60 margin-bottom 14px · four 14px #3C3E42 rows on a
 *                   9px inter-row margin
 *       cell 3      padding 40px 30px · same heading · three 14px #3C3E42 rows
 *     colophon      padding 20px 34px, flex space-between, gap 16px, wrap,
 *                   mono 9.5px/.18em/uppercase #5A5C60, two cells
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string in this file, and nothing else. Each is
 * declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each is a hook a
 * sidebar input actually owns (a data-field on any other path is a dead hook
 * no input can reach):
 *   footer.brand                     — the 22px wordmark, and the colophon's
 *                                      identity half (HeaderBN's wordmark too:
 *                                      one field, three places)
 *   footer.locality                  — the rust mono place tag under it
 *   footer.blurb                     — the 36ch standing line
 *   navCtaText                       — the one action's label (see D3)
 *   navCtaHref                       — its target (data-href-field)
 *   footer.explore.title             — the links column heading
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.text   — a links-column row, when the owner
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.href     filled the footer's own list
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — a links-column row, when the column is
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href             mirroring the masthead instead (D2)
 *   footer.visit.title               — the contact column heading
 *   location.phone                   — the tel: row (data-field only, D6)
 *   contact.email                    — the mailto: row (D7)
 *   location.address                 — the address line, and the colophon's
 *                                      other half
 *   footer.notes.${i}                — the colophon's RIGHT cell (D1)
 *
 * DERIVED here, never authored here:
 *   · the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when footer.brand is
 *     blank — the same name out of the same submission, not an invention, and
 *     the same fallback the schema itself declares.
 *   · the blurb falls back to footer.brand_blurb, the schema's own alias.
 *   · phone, email and address fall back to layout.contact.* — which IS
 *     content.contact (lib/astro-builder.ts passes it straight through with a
 *     display-formatted phone), so an owner who filled the footer group's own
 *     "Email" / "Address" boxes still gets those rows, and no two sections on
 *     this page can print different numbers or different addresses.
 *   · the tel: href is built from the phone's own digits and the mailto: href
 *     from the email. Neither prefix is ever stored.
 *   · every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBN really
 *     renders. PAGE_ANCHORS, ANCHOR_ALIAS and the helper are byte-identical to
 *     HeaderBN's, so the masthead and the colophon can never send a visitor to
 *     two different places.
 *   · `i` on every list row is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index (R6), so dropping
 *     an unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3. Nothing in this
 *     footer is numbered on screen, so no display index is needed here.
 *   · the colophon's address is FLATTENED to one line (newlines to commas) to
 *     fit a single row of tracked caps — which is why that node, and only that
 *     node, carries data-derived: components/editor/editorBridge.ts skips
 *     data-derived nodes when it paints a live edit back, and would otherwise
 *     write the raw multi-line scalar into a caps row. The COLUMN's address is
 *     NOT derived: it is the stored string verbatim, broken by
 *     `white-space: pre-line`, so an inline edit there stays truthful.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Panday Coffee Works", "Roastery & bar — Escolta,
 *     Manila", the blurb, "Sections", "Price list", "The works", "Gallery",
 *     "Notes", "Contact", "Contact us", "+63 917 555 0108", "4 Escolta St,
 *     Binondo, Manila" and "Prices in Philippine pesos" are the DESIGNER
 *     filling in their own template. Not one of those words is in this file. A
 *     roastery that supplied no links gets no links column, not the designer's
 *     four.
 *   · THE PREFIX OF THE DESIGN'S PLACE TAG. Its `shopMeta` is the literal
 *     'Roastery & bar — ' concatenated with the town. Only the TOWN is owner
 *     data, so only the town is printed — footer.locality, alone, in the
 *     designer's exact mono metrics. HeaderBN drops the same prefix for the
 *     same reason. Printing it would put a trade description on a business that
 *     never claimed one.
 *   · a column heading with no column under it, a lock with no name, a blurb
 *     with no text, a link with no label or no reachable target, an action with
 *     no label, a colophon with nothing in it, and the whole footer when all of
 *     the above are empty. EVERY guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value
 *     removes the ELEMENT and not just its text — no empty rule, no heading
 *     over nothing, no hairline under a row that is not there.
 *   · THE GRID'S CLOSING HAIRLINE when nothing follows it (see D5).
 *   · footer.hours and footer.social. Both are declared in this schema group,
 *     and the design's footer draws neither an hours block nor a social run —
 *     LocationBN owns the hours. Inventing two blocks the design never drew is
 *     not this file's call.
 *   · .reveal. The footer is the page terminus; fading in the last thing on
 *     screen buys nothing and risks a footer that never un-hides.
 *   · any star row, rating or numeral. R2's star rule has nothing to bite on
 *     here: there is not one node in this footer that could print a claim the
 *     owner did not make. There is no upload either, hence no role="img", no
 *     .plate and no image alt text.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER WOULD OTHERWISE TAKE FOR AN OVERSIGHT
 *
 * D1. THE RAIL IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. Every railed section on this
 *     page carries a rotated index — the design numbers twelve of them, 00 to
 *     11, ending at "11 / Contact" on the CTA band. The FOOTER's rail is drawn
 *     with its hairline and nothing in it. That is deliberate rather than an
 *     omission: the spine runs to the bottom of the sheet, but the colophon is
 *     not a numbered section OF the document — it is the imprint under it, and
 *     a technical document does not index its own colophon. So this file emits
 *     .rail with no .rail-t, and prints no thirteenth index. FLAG FOR REVIEW:
 *     the section brief asks for the number matching this file's position in
 *     the render order ("12 / …"). The design is the specification and the
 *     design leaves it blank, so blank is what shipped — say the word and the
 *     label goes in, but the WORD beside the numeral would then be one this
 *     file authored, which is the second reason it is absent.
 *     Consequence handled: the wrapper's 760 pass turns .rail from a vertical
 *     column into a horizontal bar with 12px of padding and a --line rule under
 *     it. An EMPTY one would be a blank ruled strip above the footer on every
 *     phone — a hairline under a row that is not there. So the rail is hidden
 *     at exactly 760px, where the wrapper flips it, and not one pixel earlier:
 *     from 761px up it is still the vertical spine and it stays.
 *
 * D2. THE LINKS COLUMN HAS TWO SOURCES AND TWO HOOK SHAPES.
 *     footer.explore.links is the schema's own list for this column, so it wins
 *     when the owner filled it. Otherwise the column mirrors the masthead,
 *     reading content.navbar_links — and note it is CONTENT, not
 *     layout.navLinks, for the reason HeaderBN states at length: lib/astro-
 *     builder.ts fills content.navbar_links from the owner's rows or from
 *     derive-content-defaults' five, but builds layout.navLinks from the RAW
 *     content.navbar_links || its own four, so on a site whose owner supplied
 *     none the two arrays differ in length and in labels. `navbar_links.${i}`
 *     is only a live hook when `i` indexes THAT array. layout.navLinks is kept
 *     as a defensive fallback for a harness that renders this component without
 *     going through astro-builder — the same fallback, in the same order, as
 *     HeaderBN's, so the two link lists cannot disagree.
 *
 * D3. THE ACTION IS DRAWN ONCE, NOT TWICE. The design draws contactLabel in
 *     BOTH the brand column and the contact column, and that reads correctly
 *     there because the designer set contactLabel to "Contact us" and headed
 *     the column "Contact". On a real site both come from
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts, which seeds navCtaText to "Get in touch"
 *     — so ONE field would print the same three words twice, six lines apart.
 *     The rule here:
 *       · the action rides the BRAND column whenever that column exists on its
 *         own merits (a wordmark, a place tag or a blurb);
 *       · it falls to the CONTACT column only when the brand column does not
 *         render at all — which is where the design also draws it;
 *       · and never in the contact column when it merely restates that
 *         column's heading, compared case- and whitespace-insensitively,
 *         because "Get In Touch" is the same stutter.
 *     The brand gate counts only the lock and the blurb, NOT ctaText, so the
 *     fallback branch stays reachable (folding ctaText into that gate is what
 *     made the equivalent branch dead code in an earlier family).
 *     The pair hooked is navCtaText / navCtaHref, from the Header group, not
 *     hero.cta1: components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle by
 *     counting hooks of a given prefix inside closest('section,footer'), so a
 *     footer carrying hero.* hooks becomes a candidate for "hide the Hero
 *     block". navCtaText has no entry in that prefix map at all, so it is
 *     toggle-inert. HeaderBN hooks the same pair for the same reason.
 *
 * D4. NO .btn IN THIS FILE, AT ALL (R4). lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 *     `.testi .btn, .cta-band .btn, footer .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }`
 *     — so a .btn anywhere under this <footer> would take a label colour picked
 *     for an INK ground and could vanish on this bone one. The design's footer
 *     has no button anyway (one 10.5px text action and 14px rows), so the
 *     action is the wrapper's .linkc and the rows are plain anchors, neither of
 *     which the engine touches. None of the forbidden names (.btn-primary,
 *     .btn-ghost, .nav-cta, .cta-band, .trust, .testi) appears either, and
 *     .linkc's own font-size is restated to the design's 10.5px — its 11px is
 *     the only value of the primitive this file changes, and the 44px tap
 *     floor it brings is kept.
 *
 * D5. THE CLOSING HAIRLINE RIDES A MODIFIER. The design draws that rule
 *     BETWEEN the grid and the colophon, so .pcbn-fgrid--ruled is added only
 *     when a colophon follows. Left unconditional it becomes an empty rule at
 *     the bottom of the page whenever brand, address and notes are all blank
 *     while a column still renders. The cells' vertical hairlines are drawn
 *     with :not(:last-child) for the same reason: with one column rendering,
 *     an unconditional border-right would rule off nothing.
 *
 * D6. THE tel: ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY, no data-href-field. Its href is
 *     derived from the field's own value, so a href hook there would write
 *     "tel:+63…" straight back into the phone box. Same for the mailto: row.
 *
 * D7. THE EMAIL ROW IS AN ADDITION. The design draws none — its demo roastery
 *     took orders on Messenger — but contact.email is schema-declared in the
 *     very group this footer belongs to, and no other BN section binds it, so a
 *     business that supplies one had it appear NOWHERE on the page. It takes
 *     the design's own 14px row treatment, so nothing new is invented visually.
 *
 * D8. PHONE AND EMAIL ARE UNDERLINED, THE ADDRESS IS NOT. The design draws all
 *     three at 14px --ink-2 inside one column, so the two that are LINKS and
 *     the one that is not would be identical in family, size, weight and
 *     colour — nothing at all would mark the tappable rows, which is worse
 *     than the colour-only cue WCAG 1.4.1 already rules out. The underline is
 *     the distinction. text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover
 *     cannot shift the rows. Deliberately NOT applied to the links column:
 *     those are wayfinding links in a list, unambiguous by position and by
 *     heading, and the design draws them bare.
 *
 * D9. NO TOP HAIRLINE, AND NO BOTTOM ONE. Every other seam on this page is a
 *     rule, so both absences look like omissions. Neither is: the CtaBand above
 *     already closes itself with `border-bottom:1px #17181A`, so the band edge
 *     IS the divider and a second rule would double it; and the design's footer
 *     declares no bottom border at all, because there is nothing under it to
 *     divide from. The wrapper's .sec primitive adds one, so this file turns it
 *     off — the ONE thing it overrides on .sec, and it does not touch
 *     grid-template-columns, which would otherwise beat the wrapper's own 760
 *     collapse from outside a media query.
 *
 * D10. THE ROWS ARE BLOCKS, NOT THE DESIGN'S <p>. The design uses
 *      `<p style="margin:0 0 9px">` per row, last row margin:0, over a body
 *      with no declared line-height — 14px at Archivo's `normal` line box plus
 *      the 9px margin, so a pitch a shade under 26px. Here each row is a block
 *      anchor at 14px/1.5 with 3px of padding either side: 21 + 3 + 3 = 27px,
 *      one pixel proud of the design rather than equal to it. In exchange the
 *      whole row is the tap target and no trailing margin dangles above the
 *      closing hairline.
 *
 * D11. THE LEADING CELL IS ALWAYS PADDED 34px ON THE LEFT. The design gives the
 *      brand cell 34px and the other two 30px, which puts the brand cell's text
 *      on the same left edge as the colophon's. With no brand column that
 *      alignment would break by 4px, so the padding-left follows the FIRST
 *      RENDERED cell rather than the brand class. When all three render this is
 *      the design's own value on the design's own cell — identical pixels.
 *
 * D12. THE COLOPHON'S RIGHT CELL IS footer.notes, NOT THE CURRENCY SENTENCE.
 *      The design draws `Prices in {{ currencyName }}` there. That is a claim
 *      about how this business quotes, and no field carries it, so the sentence
 *      is gone — but the CELL is not, because footer.notes is declared in this
 *      very group, labelled "Footer notes (copyright row)", and
 *      lib/derive-content-defaults.ts seeds it to `© <year> <name>` from the
 *      owner's own submitted name (and deliberately suppresses a bare "© 2026"
 *      with no name). So the design's two-cell row survives with the owner's
 *      own small print in it. A note that repeats the left cell VERBATIM is
 *      dropped — an exact-match test only, because "© 2026 Panday" is different
 *      information from "Panday · 4 Escolta St" and both belong on the row.
 *
 * D13. LINE-HEIGHT IS RESTATED EVERYWHERE, AND NOT AT THE DESIGN'S FIGURE. The
 *      design's body declares no line-height at all, so every string in its
 *      footer renders at `normal` — the wordmark, the place tag, both column
 *      headings, every row and the colophon. The blurb's 1.8 is the ONE value
 *      it writes down, and that one is reproduced verbatim. Inheriting is not
 *      an option for the rest: the wrapper's body sets 1.62, so a value has to
 *      be stated either way, and `normal` would not be the design's intent so
 *      much as the design's silence. What is stated is a readable line box for
 *      a string whose length this file does not control — 1.15 wordmark, 1.5
 *      place tag, 1.4 heading, 1.5 row and address line, 1.9 colophon. In the
 *      design each of those is a single demo line and the value never shows;
 *      on a real site any of them can wrap, and `normal` under 9.5px caps at
 *      .18em puts two tracked lines almost on top of each other.
 *
 * COLOUR — wrapper tokens only, no literal hex anywhere (R1). Every colour the
 * design's footer uses already has a token holding the same value:
 *   #EFEEEA --paper (the ground, inherited from body) · #C8C7C2 --line (every
 *   hairline: the rail's, the cells', the closing one) · #17181A --ink (the
 *   wordmark) · #A8351B --accent (the place tag and the action) · #3C3E42
 *   --ink-2 (the blurb, every link row AND the address line — the design does
 *   not grey that line off) · #5A5C60 --muted (the two column headings and the
 *   colophon caps, and nothing else) · #7C2412 --accent-deep
 *   (the design's OWN hover: its stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#7C2412},
 *   shadowed in the demo only by the inline colour on each footer link, so
 *   shipping no hover would be an interaction regression dressed as fidelity)
 *   · 0 --r, because nothing here is round. Nothing needed re-deriving and
 *   nothing was invented.
 *
 * CONTRAST — COMPUTED for this file from the hexes PageBN declares (sRGB
 * relative luminance, WCAG 2.x), not estimated, and not copied from the
 * wrapper's table:
 *   --ink on --paper           15.30  (wordmark)
 *   --ink-2 on --paper          9.23  (blurb, link rows, address line)
 *   --accent-deep on --paper    8.54  (row hover)
 *   --muted on --paper          5.77  (column headings, colophon)
 *   --accent on --paper         5.67  (place tag, the action)
 * The smallest type on this band is the 9.5px colophon at 5.77 — WCAG's 3:1
 * large-text allowance does not apply at that size, and 5.77 clears the 4.5:1
 * normal-text bar without needing it. Every run here is AA or better.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE — this file owns its OWN grid; the wrapper owns the shared 760 and
 * 520 pass. There is NO clamp() anywhere in it, so the floor-under-a-cap
 * inversion two earlier audits found is not expressible here. Every value is a
 * fixed px or em, and here is the whole ladder traced across both queries
 * (base → 900 → 520):
 *   cell padding        40/30 → 32/26 → 26/18   (leading cell 34 → 26 → 18 left,
 *                                                brand cell the same on the right)
 *   wordmark            22 → 22 → 19
 *   colophon padding    20/34 → 20/26 → 16/18
 *   colophon tracking   .18em → .18em → .13em
 *   colophon leading    1.9 → 1.9 → 1.8
 *   colophon gap        16 → 16 → 10   ·   separator .45em → .45em → .3em
 * Not one type size, tracking, leading or padding GROWS as the viewport
 * narrows. Exactly two values deliberately do, and these are they: at 900 a
 * stacked link row takes min-height 44px and the address line 10px of vertical
 * padding, because a row that was one of three columns has become a primary tap
 * target. The blurb's 36ch cap is dropped at 520 — a MEASURE, not a type size:
 * it lets the standing line fill the column instead of leaving a ragged strip
 * beside it, and a max-width cannot push a page sideways.
 * Nothing may push sideways at 360px: every text node that can hold a long
 * unbroken string (a wordmark, an email, an address, a link label, a note)
 * carries overflow-wrap: anywhere — the `anywhere` value and not `break-word`,
 * because only `anywhere` also shrinks the min-content size a grid track and a
 * flex item resolve against, which is what the 900 query's flex rows need.
 * There is no full-width button in this file to need the white-space pair.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES are `pcbn-f*` — this template's own prefix, the one HeaderBN
 * already uses. An audit had to rename a whole set of classes copied verbatim
 * from a sibling: nothing in this file shares a name with FooterBM's `tcbm-f*`
 * or FooterBL's `fb-*`.
 *
 * Visibility: PageBN gates this file on visibility.footerSection — never here.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const f = c.footer || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD — byte-identical to HeaderBN's, so the masthead and the
// colophon can never send a visitor to different places.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav hrefs to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit'; lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact'. Most of those
// ids no design renders. Resolve every in-page href against what PageBN
// ACTUALLY renders: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this design
// carries the same material under another name, otherwise fall back — and the
// links column passes '' as its fallback, because a dead entry in a wayfinding
// list is worse than a missing one. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http(s) and
// anything else without a leading '#') pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames five derived targets: the services block IS the price
// list, so '#services' (and '#rooms') land on '#menu'; the roastery's own story
// absorbs both '#host' and '#why'; '#work' is the photo wall; '#location' and
// '#contact' both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. `||`, never `??`, and identical to HeaderBN's resolution:
// genericContentSchema gives footer.brand fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and
// ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand
// box still shows the business name in the sidebar. With `??` an empty string
// would be a value and the page would go wordmark-less while the sidebar
// displayed a name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real customer's own mark;
// with nothing supplied the lock is omitted instead of inventing a name.
const brand = String(f.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// One field, three consumers (the masthead tag, this tag, the map caption).
// Nothing derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent rather than
// guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(f.locality ?? '').trim();
// footer.brand_blurb is the schema's declared alias for the same box.
const blurb = String(f.blurb || f.brand_blurb || '').trim();

// The page's one action, from the Header group (D3). '#visit' is the fallback
// because that is where this design puts the address, the map and the opening
// hours, and it is the id the derived default already names.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#visit');
// Computed, never literal, and ONE conditional per attribute — an owner may
// point this at #visit or at a tel:, and forcing those into a new tab is wrong.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// Column headings — bound, not hardcoded, and guarded, so an owner who clears
// the box gets a column with no heading rather than the designer's word. The
// schema's own owner-facing labels decide which feeds which: footer.explore.title
// is "Links column heading" and footer.visit.title "Contact column heading",
// which are this design's two footer headings.
const linksTitle = String(f.explore?.title ?? '').trim();
const contactTitle = String(f.visit?.title ?? '').trim();

// ── THE LINKS COLUMN (D2). Two sources, two hook shapes, one index rule.
const exploreFromAdmin = Array.isArray(f.explore?.links) && f.explore.links.length > 0;
const navSource: any[] = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links)
  ? c.navbar_links
  : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : []);
type LinkRow = { label: string; href: string; i: number; labelHook: string; hrefHook: string };
const columnLinks: LinkRow[] = (exploreFromAdmin
  ? f.explore.links.map((l: any) => ({ label: l?.text, href: l?.href }))
  : navSource
).map((l: any, i: number): LinkRow => ({
  label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(),
  href: onPage(l?.href, ''),
  i,
  labelHook: exploreFromAdmin ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.text` : `navbar_links.${i}.label`,
  hrefHook: exploreFromAdmin ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.href` : `navbar_links.${i}.href`,
})).filter((l: LinkRow) => Boolean(l.label && l.href));

// Contact rows. layout.contact IS content.contact (astro-builder passes it
// through with a display-formatted phone), so these fallbacks also pick up the
// footer group's own "Email" / "Address" boxes, and no two sections on this
// page can print different numbers or different addresses.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || lay.contact?.phone || c.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const email = String(c.contact?.email || lay.contact?.email || '').trim();
const address = String(c.location?.address || lay.contact?.address || c.contact?.address || '').trim();
// Built, never stored (D6).
const phoneDigits = phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '');
// Colophon identity line: the design prints one line, and location.address is a
// multi-line textarea. Flattened for THIS row only, hence data-derived on it.
const addressFlat = address.replace(/\s*\n+\s*/g, ', ');

const norm = (v: string): string => v.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().toLowerCase();

// Colophon right cell (D12). Empty rows go, and so does a note that is an EXACT
// repeat of what the left cell already prints — nothing cleverer, because a ©
// line that happens to contain the name is different information from the
// name-and-address line beside it. The ORIGINAL index travels with the survivor.
type NoteRow = { v: string; i: number };
const notes: NoteRow[] = (Array.isArray(f.notes) ? f.notes : [])
  .map((n: any, i: number): NoteRow => ({ v: String(n ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((n: NoteRow) => Boolean(n.v) && norm(n.v) !== norm(brand) && norm(n.v) !== norm(addressFlat));

// ── GATES — BODY content only (R5). The two column headings never count: a
// heading is not a column, and one printed over nothing is the exact failure
// that rule exists for. footer.brand / footer.blurb / navbar_links / navCtaText
// / footer.notes ARE seeded by derive-content-defaults, but from the owner's own
// submitted name, city and business type — that is their data, not an invented
// headline, and none of them is a tag or a display heading.
const hasBrandCol = Boolean(brand || locality || blurb);
// D3: one action, and never a stutter against the heading beside it.
const brandShowsCta = Boolean(ctaText) && hasBrandCol;
const showContactCta =
  Boolean(ctaText) && !brandShowsCta && norm(ctaText) !== norm(contactTitle);
const hasLinksCol = columnLinks.length > 0;
const hasContactCol = Boolean(showContactCta || phone || email || address);
const colCount = [hasBrandCol, hasLinksCol, hasContactCol].filter(Boolean).length;
const hasGrid = colCount > 0;
const hasColophon = Boolean(brand || addressFlat || notes.length > 0);
// ONE modifier, never a compound selector — the 900px collapse has to out-rank
// whichever of these is on the element, and a `.a.b` pair would need a `.a.b.c`
// rule there to beat it.
const gridMod =
  colCount === 3 ? 'pcbn-fgrid--wide3'
  : colCount === 2 ? (hasBrandCol ? 'pcbn-fgrid--wide2' : 'pcbn-fgrid--even2')
  : 'pcbn-fgrid--one';
---
{(hasGrid || hasColophon) && (
<footer class="sec pcbn-foot">
  {/* THE SPINE. .sec and .rail are PageBN's primitives, used rather than
      rebuilt. The rail is EMPTY because the design draws it empty — the
      hairline runs to the bottom of the sheet, but the colophon carries no
      index (D1). It is hidden at 760px, where the wrapper turns it horizontal
      and an empty one would rule off nothing. */}
  <div class="rail pcbn-fspine"></div>
  <div class="pcbn-fmain">
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class:list={['pcbn-fgrid', gridMod, hasColophon && 'pcbn-fgrid--ruled']}>
        {hasBrandCol && (
          <div class="pcbn-fcell pcbn-fbrand">
            {brand && <span class="pcbn-fmark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {/* Only the TOWN — the design's 'Roastery & bar — ' prefix is a
                trade description no field carries. */}
            {locality && <span class="pcbn-fmeta" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
            {blurb && <p class="pcbn-fblurb" data-field="footer.blurb">{blurb}</p>}
            {/* Both hooks sit on the ANCHOR: the editor's link popover reads
                data-field from whatever carries data-href-field, so a label on
                an inner span would leave it editing an empty path. The arrow is
                generated content — editorBridge writes an inline edit back
                through innerHTML, so a glyph inside the element could end up
                stored in navCtaText; a pseudo-element cannot. */}
            {brandShowsCta && (
              <a
                class="linkc pcbn-fcta"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasLinksCol && (
          <div class="pcbn-fcell">
            {linksTitle && <h4 class="pcbn-fh" data-field="footer.explore.title">{linksTitle}</h4>}
            {columnLinks.map((l) => (
              <a class="pcbn-frow" href={l.href} data-field={l.labelHook} data-href-field={l.hrefHook}>{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasContactCol && (
          <div class="pcbn-fcell">
            {contactTitle && <h4 class="pcbn-fh" data-field="footer.visit.title">{contactTitle}</h4>}
            {showContactCta && (
              <a
                class="pcbn-frow"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {(phone || email || address) && (
              <address class="pcbn-faddr">
                {phone && (
                  <a class="pcbn-frow pcbn-fchan" href={`tel:${phoneDigits}`} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
                )}
                {/* mailto: is BUILT, never stored — the visible text stays the
                    plain address so an inline edit can never write "mailto:…"
                    back into the email box. */}
                {email && (
                  <a class="pcbn-frow pcbn-fchan" href={`mailto:${email}`} data-field="contact.email">{email}</a>
                )}
                {/* The stored string verbatim — `white-space: pre-line` draws
                    the owner's own line breaks, so the DOM text and the field
                    value stay identical and a live edit cannot lie. */}
                {address && <span class="pcbn-fline" data-field="location.address">{address}</span>}
              </address>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    {hasColophon && (
      <div class="mono pcbn-fcolo">
        {(brand || addressFlat) && (
          <span class="pcbn-fid">
            {brand && <span data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {brand && addressFlat && <span class="pcbn-fsep" aria-hidden="true">—</span>}
            {addressFlat && <span data-field="location.address" data-derived>{addressFlat}</span>}
          </span>
        )}
        {notes.length > 0 && (
          <span class="pcbn-fnotes">
            {notes.map((n, d) => (
              <>
                {d > 0 && <span class="pcbn-fsep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
                <span data-field={`footer.notes.${n.i}`}>{n.v}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</footer>
)}
<style>
  /* Scoped, single block, no is:global. The design's colophon: the page's own
     bone ground, the spine continuing down the left, and structure drawn with
     RULES rather than gaps.
     (Never write a literal end-style tag inside this block, comments included:
      it is an HTML raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet
      right there, silently truncating the file.) */

  /* .sec supplies the grid and the rail width. The ONE thing overridden is its
     bottom rule: the design's footer declares none, because there is nothing
     under it to divide from (D9). grid-template-columns is deliberately NOT
     restated — a scoped rule outside a media query would out-rank the wrapper's
     own 760 collapse and freeze the rail column open on phones. */
  .pcbn-foot { border-bottom: 0; background-color: var(--paper); }

  /* .pcbn-fspine IS THE RAIL, drawn empty exactly as the design draws it (D1),
     and it has no base rule at all — deliberately. The wrapper's .rail already
     supplies the --line right hairline and the full-height stretch, so the
     class exists only to be switched off in the 760 query at the bottom of this
     block, where the wrapper flips the rail horizontal and an empty one would
     rule off nothing.

     .pcbn-fmain is the design's right column: a bare div with no padding of its
     own. min-width:0 is the whole rule — without it a long unbroken address or
     link label sets the grid item's automatic minimum to its own min-content
     width and pushes the page sideways. */
  .pcbn-fmain { min-width: 0; }

  /* The design's 1.6fr / 1fr / 1fr, at NO gap: the cells are told apart by
     their hairlines, which is what makes this read as a ruled sheet. minmax(0,…)
     on every track — the design's own value — so a long unbroken address or
     link label cannot widen a column past its share and push the page sideways.
     One modifier per rendered-column count, so a two-column footer does not
     leave a third of the sheet blank (D5). */
  .pcbn-fgrid { display: grid; }
  .pcbn-fgrid--wide3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pcbn-fgrid--wide2 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pcbn-fgrid--even2 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pcbn-fgrid--one { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  /* The rule the design draws between the grid and the colophon — carried by a
     modifier so it can never close a page that has no colophon under it. */
  .pcbn-fgrid--ruled { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }

  /* The design's cells: 40px 30px, opened by a hairline on every one but the
     last. The leading cell always takes the design's 34px so its text sits on
     the same left edge as the colophon (D11). */
  .pcbn-fcell { padding: 40px 30px; min-width: 0; }
  .pcbn-fcell:first-child { padding-left: 34px; }
  .pcbn-fcell:not(:last-child) { border-right: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .pcbn-fbrand { padding-right: 34px; }

  /* 800 at 22px, -.02em, uppercase — the design's own footer wordmark. It is
     three pixels LARGER than the masthead's 19px lock and identical to it in
     tracking: the design writes -.02em on both, so size is the only thing that
     separates them. It is a <span>, so the wrapper's heading reset never
     touches it and every value is stated here. A footer wordmark may WRAP (the
     masthead ellipses instead, because that bar is one row tall); overflow-wrap
     keeps a single unbroken word inside the cell, and the line-height is stated
     for the reason D13 gives. */
  .pcbn-fmark {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 800; font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -.02em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink); min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* NOT the wrapper's .mono primitive: that is 500 weight at .2em in --muted,
     and the design's tag is 400 at .16em in --accent. Close enough to be
     mistaken for it, far enough to be visibly wrong if borrowed. */
  .pcbn-fmeta {
    display: block; margin: 7px 0 15px;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 400; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.5; min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Qualified by the parent class on purpose. The wrapper's paragraph reset is
     `html[data-page="BN"] :where(p)` — one type selector plus one attribute, so
     (0,1,1); the :where() contributes nothing. astro.config.mjs leaves
     scopedStyleStrategy at the Astro 5 default of 'attribute', so an unqualified
     scoped class here would compile to `.pcbn-fblurb[data-astro-cid-…]` at
     (0,2,0) and would in fact clear the reset on its own. The parent class is
     kept anyway: it costs nothing and it does not depend on that config staying
     put, where 'where' scoping would drop the same rule to (0,1,0) and lose the
     margin. 36ch, 14px/1.8 and the 16px bottom margin are the design's own. */
  .pcbn-fbrand .pcbn-fblurb {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 36ch; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* .linkc already carries the family, the tracking, the uppercasing, the rust
     and the 44px tap floor. Only the design's 10.5px and the arrow are added —
     the arrow as generated content, with no margin: .linkc is an inline-flex
     box, so the pseudo-element is a flex ITEM and the primitive's own
     `gap: .5em` already spaces it. A margin would stack on top of that gap. */
  .pcbn-fcta { font-size: 10.5px; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .pcbn-fcta::after { content: '\2192'; }

  /* Column headings: mono 10px caps at .2em in --muted, the design's own
     treatment. They are <h4> because two real headings give the columns a
     structure a screen-reader user can navigate, where the design's plain <div>
     does not — which means every declaration has to be restated, because
     `html[data-page="BN"] :where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` scores (0,1,1) — a type selector
     plus an attribute, the :where() adding nothing — and pins family, weight,
     colour, tracking, line-height, transform and margin. The rule below lands at
     (0,2,0) under the project's 'attribute' scoping and clears it. It is NOT the
     .kick eyebrow: that primitive is rust at .24em and belongs to the sections;
     the design's footer headings are grey at .2em and nothing more. */
  .pcbn-fh {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 400; font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.4; letter-spacing: .2em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's rows: 14px --ink-2 on a ~27px pitch. Blocks rather than the
     design's paragraphs, so the whole row is the tap target and the last row
     leaves no margin dangling above the closing hairline (D10). --accent-deep
     is the design's own stated hover. */
  .pcbn-frow {
    display: block; padding: 3px 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    transition: color .18s ease;
  }
  .pcbn-frow:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .pcbn-frow { transition: none; } }

  /* The address block. The design gives its address line the SAME 14px
     --ink-2 as the link rows above it — it is not greyed off — so the block
     simply carries that colour and the two channel rows inside it inherit it
     through .pcbn-frow. <address> for the semantics; font-style reset because
     the UA italicises it and this design has no italic. */
  .pcbn-faddr {
    font-style: normal; margin: 0; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  /* The stored string, unaltered — pre-line draws the owner's own line breaks
     without this file generating a single line-break tag. */
  .pcbn-fline { display: block; padding: 3px 0; white-space: pre-line; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* Phone and email are underlined; the address beside them is not (D8).
     text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover cannot shift rows. */
  .pcbn-faddr .pcbn-fchan {
    text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .pcbn-faddr .pcbn-fchan:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent-deep); text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  /* The colophon: 20px 34px, two cells, space-between, wrapping rather than
     overflowing. .mono supplies the family, the uppercasing and --muted; the
     design's 9.5px, its .18em tracking and its 400 weight are restated, because
     the primitive is 10px/.2em/500 and would quietly thicken the row. The
     leading is the one figure here that is NOT the design's — see D13. With one
     cell absent the remaining cell simply sits left. */
  .pcbn-fcolo {
    padding: 20px 34px;
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px;
    font-weight: 400; font-size: 9.5px; letter-spacing: .18em; line-height: 1.9;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcbn-fid, .pcbn-fnotes { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .pcbn-fsep { margin: 0 .45em; }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 pass; this file owns its own grid.
     Three columns cannot hold 14px rows and a 36ch blurb below ~900px, so they
     stack at ONE width — there is never a band where a column is 90px wide. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Every count modifier is listed so the collapse out-ranks whichever one is
       on the element; each compiles at the same specificity and later in source
       order. */
    .pcbn-fgrid,
    .pcbn-fgrid--wide3,
    .pcbn-fgrid--wide2,
    .pcbn-fgrid--even2,
    .pcbn-fgrid--one { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    /* The hairline turns from an opening border-right into a ruled underline,
       which is how every other stacked list in this template is drawn — and it
       is still :not(:last-child), so the final cell is not ruled twice against
       the grid's own closing rule. */
    .pcbn-fcell { padding: 32px 26px; }
    .pcbn-fcell:first-child { padding-left: 26px; }
    .pcbn-fbrand { padding-right: 26px; }
    .pcbn-fcell:not(:last-child) {
      border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    /* Stacked, each row is a primary target rather than one of three columns. */
    .pcbn-frow { min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; }
    .pcbn-fline { padding: 10px 0; }
    .pcbn-fcolo { padding: 20px 26px; }
  }

  /* 760 is where the WRAPPER flips .rail from a vertical column to a horizontal
     bar with a rule under it. This rail is empty, so at that exact width it
     becomes a blank ruled strip — it goes instead (D1). Above 760 it is still
     the spine and it stays. */
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .pcbn-fspine { display: none; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .pcbn-fcell { padding: 26px 18px; }
    .pcbn-fcell:first-child { padding-left: 18px; }
    .pcbn-fbrand { padding-right: 18px; }
    .pcbn-fmark { font-size: 19px; }
    .pcbn-fbrand .pcbn-fblurb { max-width: none; }
    /* Wide tracking on a full-width caps row is what actually pushes a 360px
       screen sideways here, so the tracking comes in rather than the type. */
    .pcbn-fcolo { padding: 16px 18px; letter-spacing: .13em; gap: 10px; line-height: 1.8; }
    .pcbn-fsep { margin: 0 .3em; }
  }
</style>
